window.docContents[162].resolve({"/#getting-to-know-types":{"contents":"Types classify programs based on the values that they can\ncompute. Types serve a number of roles in a program:\n\n1. They allow the compiler to make decisions about the in-memory representation of a value.2. They help programmers to communicate their intent to others, serving as a lightweight specification for the inputs and outputs of a function.\n    The compiler ensures that the program adheres to this specification.3. They prevent various potential mistakes, such as adding a number to a string, and thus reduce the number of tests that are necessary for a program.4. They help the Lean compiler automate the production of auxiliary code that can save boilerplate.\n\nLean's type system is unusually expressive.\nTypes can encode strong specifications like “this sorting function returns a permutation of its input” and flexible specifications like “this function has different return types, depending on the value of its argument”.\nThe type system can even be used as a full-blown logic for proving mathematical theorems.\nThis cutting-edge expressive power doesn't make simpler types unnecessary, however, and understanding these simpler types is a prerequisite for using the more advanced features.\n\nEvery program in Lean must have a type. In particular, every\nexpression must have a type before it can be evaluated. In the\nexamples so far, Lean has been able to discover a type on its own, but\nit is sometimes necessary to provide one. This is done using the colon\noperator inside parentheses:Here,  is the type of natural numbers, which are arbitrary-precision unsigned integers.\nIn Lean,  is the default type for non-negative integer literals.\nThis default type is not always the best choice.\nIn C, unsigned integers underflow to the largest representable numbers when subtraction would otherwise yield a result less than zero.\n, however, can represent arbitrarily-large unsigned numbers, so there is no largest number to underflow to.\nThus, subtraction on  returns  when the answer would have otherwise been negative.\nFor instance,evaluates to  rather than -1.\nTo use a type that can represent the negative integers, provide it directly:With this type, the result is , as expected.\n\nTo check the type of an expression without evaluating it, use #check instead of #eval. For instance:reports  without actually performing the subtraction.\n\nWhen a program can't be given a type, an error is returned from both #check and #eval. For instance:outputsbecause the first argument to  is expected to be a string, but a list of strings was provided instead.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Getting to Know Lean","header":"1.2. Types","id":"/#getting-to-know-types"},"/#indexed-families-exercises":{"contents":"Getting a feel for programming with dependent types requires experience, and the exercises in this section are very important.\nFor each exercise, try to see which mistakes the type checker can catch, and which ones it can't, by experimenting with the code as you go.\nThis is also a good way to develop a feel for the error messages.\n\n* Double-check that  gives the right answer when combining the three highest peaks in Oregon with the three highest peaks in Denmark.\n   Because  doesn't have the syntactic sugar that  has, it can be helpful to begin by defining  and .* Define a function  with type .* Define a function  that combines the entries in a  one at a time with a function.\n   It should have the type .* Define a function  that splits a  of pairs into a pair of s. It should have the type .* Define a function  that adds an entry to the end of a . Its type should be  and  should yield .* Define a function  that reverses the order of a .* Define a function  with the following type: .\n   Verify that it works by checking that  yields .* Define a function  with type  that returns the first  entries in the . Check that it works on an example.\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Programming with Dependent Types\u0009Indexed Families","header":"7.1.1. Exercises","id":"/#indexed-families-exercises"}});